x86_64-linux  A closure type has no name the standard obliges anybody to match - [expr.prim.lambda] calls it a unique unnamed type - and the two compilers number theirs differently. Both write `$_N` within the enclosing function, and cxx1 numbers them in the order they are written where clang does not: measured on a file whose third lambda came out `$_0`, so the order is clang's own and not source order. Nothing links against these: the call operator of a closure is reached through the object, and the object never leaves the function that made it. The names of everything else in this case agree, and x86_64-windows and arm64-darwin agree on all of it.
